{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Your adapter owns its schema. TanStack AI never inspects your tables, so you\ndecide the table layout and how schema changes are applied. Apply those changes\nbefore deploying code that reads or writes the corresponding stores."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"create-tables-on-open-for-local-development","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Create tables on open, for local development"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A hand-rolled adapter can create its tables the first time it opens the database.\nThe SQLite example in "},{"type":"link","href":"./build-your-own-adapter","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Build your own adapter"}]},{"type":"text","value":" does this\nbehind a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"migrate"},{"type":"text","value":" flag with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS"},{"type":"text","value":", so it is idempotent:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { sqlitePersistence } from './sqlite-persistence'\n\nconst persistence = sqlitePersistence({\n  url: 'file:.data/state.sqlite',\n  migrate: true,\n})","lang":"ts","meta":"ignore"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is convenient for local development and tests. Avoid request-time migrations\nin production."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"apply-migrations-in-production","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Apply migrations in production"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In production, run schema changes through your normal deployment workflow, not on\nfirst request. Keep the DDL for the four tables ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"messages"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runs"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interrupts"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"metadata"},{"type":"text","value":") in a versioned migration and apply it with the same\ntool you use for the rest of your database, before the new code ships."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you build the adapter with an ORM or query builder, let that tool own the\nmigration journal. A Drizzle schema drives "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"drizzle-kit"},{"type":"text","value":"; a Prisma models\nfragment drives "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"prisma migrate"},{"type":"text","value":"; a raw SQL adapter checks in plain "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".sql"},{"type":"text","value":" files.\nThe adapter-building skills in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-persistence"},{"type":"text","value":" cover each of these\nworkflows."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"an-existing-schema-owns-its-own-migrations","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An existing schema owns its own migrations"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you map the store contracts onto tables you already have (see\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./build-your-own-adapter#existing-database-map-the-contracts-onto-your-schema","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Build your own adapter"}]},{"type":"text","value":"),\nthose tables are part of your application schema, and your existing migration tool\nalready owns them. Add the columns the stores need in a normal migration. Keep any\nextra app-owned columns nullable or defaulted so the stores' inserts still\nsucceed."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"upgrade-discipline","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Upgrade discipline"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep the DDL for the store tables in a reviewable migration, not inline in\nrequest handlers."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Back up production state where required."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Apply migrations before deploying code that depends on them."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep rollback and partial-deployment behavior explicit."}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"create-tables-on-open-for-local-development","text":"Create tables on open, for local development","level":2},{"id":"apply-migrations-in-production","text":"Apply migrations in production","level":2},{"id":"an-existing-schema-owns-its-own-migrations","text":"An existing schema owns its own migrations","level":2},{"id":"upgrade-discipline","text":"Upgrade discipline","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nYour adapter owns its schema. TanStack AI never inspects your tables, so you\ndecide the table layout and how schema changes are applied. Apply those changes\nbefore deploying code that reads or writes the corresponding stores.\n\n## Create tables on open, for local development\n\nA hand-rolled adapter can create its tables the first time it opens the database.\nThe SQLite example in [Build your own adapter](./build-your-own-adapter) does this\nbehind a `migrate` flag with `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS`, so it is idempotent:\n\n```ts ignore\nimport { sqlitePersistence } from './sqlite-persistence'\n\nconst persistence = sqlitePersistence({\n  url: 'file:.data/state.sqlite',\n  migrate: true,\n})\n```\n\nThis is convenient for local development and tests. Avoid request-time migrations\nin production.\n\n## Apply migrations in production\n\nIn production, run schema changes through your normal deployment workflow, not on\nfirst request. Keep the DDL for the four tables (`messages`, `runs`,\n`interrupts`, `metadata`) in a versioned migration and apply it with the same\ntool you use for the rest of your database, before the new code ships.\n\nIf you build the adapter with an ORM or query builder, let that tool own the\nmigration journal. A Drizzle schema drives `drizzle-kit`; a Prisma models\nfragment drives `prisma migrate`; a raw SQL adapter checks in plain `.sql` files.\nThe adapter-building skills in `@tanstack/ai-persistence` cover each of these\nworkflows.\n\n## An existing schema owns its own migrations\n\nIf you map the store contracts onto tables you already have (see\n[Build your own adapter](./build-your-own-adapter#existing-database-map-the-contracts-onto-your-schema)),\nthose tables are part of your application schema, and your existing migration tool\nalready owns them. Add the columns the stores need in a normal migration. Keep any\nextra app-owned columns nullable or defaulted so the stores' inserts still\nsucceed.\n\n## Upgrade discipline\n\n1. Keep the DDL for the store tables in a reviewable migration, not inline in\n   request handlers.\n2. Back up production state where required.\n3. Apply migrations before deploying code that depends on them.\n4. Keep rollback and partial-deployment behavior explicit.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Persistence Migrations\nid: migrations\n---\n\nYour adapter owns its schema. TanStack AI never inspects your tables, so you\ndecide the table layout and how schema changes are applied. Apply those changes\nbefore deploying code that reads or writes the corresponding stores.\n\n## Create tables on open, for local development\n\nA hand-rolled adapter can create its tables the first time it opens the database.\nThe SQLite example in [Build your own adapter](./build-your-own-adapter) does this\nbehind a `migrate` flag with `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS`, so it is idempotent:\n\n```ts ignore\nimport { sqlitePersistence } from './sqlite-persistence'\n\nconst persistence = sqlitePersistence({\n  url: 'file:.data/state.sqlite',\n  migrate: true,\n})\n```\n\nThis is convenient for local development and tests. Avoid request-time migrations\nin production.\n\n## Apply migrations in production\n\nIn production, run schema changes through your normal deployment workflow, not on\nfirst request. Keep the DDL for the four tables (`messages`, `runs`,\n`interrupts`, `metadata`) in a versioned migration and apply it with the same\ntool you use for the rest of your database, before the new code ships.\n\nIf you build the adapter with an ORM or query builder, let that tool own the\nmigration journal. A Drizzle schema drives `drizzle-kit`; a Prisma models\nfragment drives `prisma migrate`; a raw SQL adapter checks in plain `.sql` files.\nThe adapter-building skills in `@tanstack/ai-persistence` cover each of these\nworkflows.\n\n## An existing schema owns its own migrations\n\nIf you map the store contracts onto tables you already have (see\n[Build your own adapter](./build-your-own-adapter#existing-database-map-the-contracts-onto-your-schema)),\nthose tables are part of your application schema, and your existing migration tool\nalready owns them. Add the columns the stores need in a normal migration. Keep any\nextra app-owned columns nullable or defaulted so the stores' inserts still\nsucceed.\n\n## Upgrade discipline\n\n1. Keep the DDL for the store tables in a reviewable migration, not inline in\n   request handlers.\n2. Back up production state where required.\n3. Apply migrations before deploying code that depends on them.\n4. Keep rollback and partial-deployment behavior explicit.\n"}
